      "creator": "Edmund F. Arras",
      "credit": "This file was contributed to Wikimedia Commons by Columbus Metropolitan Library as part of a cooperation project. The donation was facilitated by the Digital Public Library of America , via its partner Ohio Digital Network . Record in source catalog DPLA identifier: d49649789513ac633fc9252021db12c4 Columbus Metropolitan Library identifier: HA3_B10_0388.tif",
      "licence": "Public domain",
      "licence_url": null,
      "source_description": "View of the water tower and Kapellbrücke, or Chapel Bridge, which spans the Reuss River in Lucerne. Constructed in the 14th century, it is is considered Europe's oldest covered bridge. The Arrases visited Lucerne during their 1913 tour of Europe. Note on slide reads \"Kapell-Brucke &light house 164 paintings.\" Edmund F. Arras (7/7/1875-10/19/1951), a prominent Columbus businessman and entrepreneur, founded one of Columbus, Ohio’s first property rental agencies in 1892. Trained as a lawyer, he graduated from OSU law school in 1896 and went on to hold positions in numerous civic groups around the city. He was particularly active within Kiwanis International and was involved with several local religious organizations. In 1913, he and his wife Elizabeth traveled to the World Sunday School Association Convention in Zurich, Switzerland. They continued on to travel extensively throughout Europe documenting their journey through photographs. Due to the timing of their trip, these photos comprise a valuable collection of images of European cities later devastated by war. The Arras family's lantern slides and negatives include images from their 1913 travels through US east coast cities, Atlantic islands of the Azores, Madeira, Gibraltar and the countries of Algeria, Italy, Switzerland, Germany, the Netherlands, Belgium, France, England, and Ireland. Also included are images from their 1920 train journey from Chicago to the western US for the Kiwanis Convention in Portland. These photographs include images of Chicago, Denver and western National Parks. Glass lantern slide of image also in collection HA3_B03_080.",
      "source_date": "1913 date QS:P571,+1913-00-00T00:00:00Z/9",
